      "id": 1,
      "text": "Argentine got its name when the post office was renamed on March 30, 1842, to avoid confusion with another office.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        1
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Argentine%2C_Michigan",
      "category": "etymology",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "A proposed railroad that would have linked Argentine to Fenton and Linden never materialized, stunting the town’s growth.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        9
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 2"
      ],
      "source": "https://99wfmk.com/argentine-genesee-county-michigan/",
      "category": "transport",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "The first settlers, Murray and William Lobdell, arrived in 1836, laying the groundwork for the community that would become Argentine.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        14
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Argentine%2C_Michigan",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "The original post office, called \"Booton,\" opened on November 1, 1837, with Murray Lobdell as its first postmaster.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        15
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Argentine%2C_Michigan",
      "category": "landmark",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "Argentine’s center sits at the intersection of Silver Lake Road and South Seymour Road, near the northwest edge of Lobdell Lake.",
